\begin{abstract}
Standard Monin--Obukhov Similarity Theory (MOST) fails under strongly stable conditions, typically overestimating nocturnal stable boundary layer (SBL) depth and failing to capture abrupt, non-equilibrium transitions \citep{nieuwstadt1984, Holtslag1993}. Building on foundational lower-dimensional bifurcation paradigms that first identified S-shaped equilibrium foldbacks \citep{mcnider1995, shi2005}, this work develops a full four-dimensional ($4\text{D}$) fast--slow dynamical system for the nocturnal SBL using Geometric Singular Perturbation Theory (GSPT; \citealp{Fenichel1979}). The framework explicitly couples fast turbulent kinetic energy (TKE, $e$) evolution with slow atmospheric rotational dynamics ($U, V$) and surface thermodynamics ($T_s$). By incorporating nonlinear Kolmogorov dissipation ($e^{3/2}/l_0$) and a smooth activation gate ($\sqrt{e+\delta}$) with a background mixing floor $\delta > 0$, we demonstrate that regime transitions—specifically the brittle shifts between weakly stable (wSBL) and very stable (vSBL) states \citep{monahan2015}—are organized by an emergent non-degenerate fold catastrophe ($\mathcal{C}_{\text{fold}}$) coupled with a transversal boundary recovery mechanism near $e = 0$. Compact, normally hyperbolic subsets of the critical manifold organize slow equilibrium dynamics in the singular limit $\varepsilon \to 0$. Frictional decoupling along the laminar sheet naturally generates Blackadar-type inertial oscillations and Low-Level Jets (LLJs) without empirical prescription. This formulation generalizes classical 1.5-order TKE closures by replacing algebraic equilibrium assumptions with explicit fast--slow manifold geometry. Observational validation using \ActiveDataset serves as the primary baseline for generated diagnostics, regime maps, and comparative sensitivity envelopes.
\end{abstract}